1
00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:01,844
You tap the Buy button.

2
00:00:02,394 --> 00:00:04,293
Seconds later, an order exists,

3
00:00:04,423 --> 00:00:06,023
and a package is on its way.

4
00:00:06,573 --> 00:00:08,066
But before that button worked,

5
00:00:08,196 --> 00:00:09,604
somebody had to plan it,

6
00:00:09,734 --> 00:00:10,630
design it,

7
00:00:10,760 --> 00:00:11,549
build it,

8
00:00:11,679 --> 00:00:12,511
test it,

9
00:00:12,641 --> 00:00:13,793
and put it online.

10
00:00:14,343 --> 00:00:19,037
That journey has a name: the software development life cycle, or SDLC.

11
00:00:19,587 --> 00:00:22,637
In this lesson, you will walk through all seven stages.

12
00:00:24,187 --> 00:00:27,387
We will follow one small online shop, called CloudCart,

13
00:00:27,517 --> 00:00:29,778
from first idea to real customer.

14
00:00:30,328 --> 00:00:31,480
Seven stages.

15
00:00:32,030 --> 00:00:32,777
Plan,

16
00:00:32,907 --> 00:00:33,824
requirements,

17
00:00:33,954 --> 00:00:34,786
design,

18
00:00:34,916 --> 00:00:35,663
build,

19
00:00:35,793 --> 00:00:36,518
test,

20
00:00:36,648 --> 00:00:37,438
deploy,

21
00:00:37,568 --> 00:00:38,528
and operate.

22
00:00:39,078 --> 00:00:41,766
Then the loop starts again, with everything we learned.

23
00:00:42,916 --> 00:00:47,865
By the end, you can name every stage, say what happens in it, and see where DevOps fits.

24
00:00:49,415 --> 00:00:50,823
Stage one: plan.

25
00:00:51,373 --> 00:00:52,888
Before anyone writes code,

26
00:00:53,018 --> 00:00:54,810
the team agrees on the problem.

27
00:00:55,360 --> 00:00:57,152
The owner wants to sell online.

28
00:00:57,702 --> 00:00:59,707
Customers want to find things fast.

29
00:01:00,257 --> 00:01:01,878
Developers need a clear goal.

30
00:01:02,428 --> 00:01:04,391
For CloudCart, the goal is simple:

31
00:01:04,941 --> 00:01:07,501
customers browse products, and place an order.

32
00:01:08,051 --> 00:01:10,888
Teams often track this work in a tool like GitHub.

33
00:01:11,438 --> 00:01:13,870
A written plan stops you building the wrong thing.

34
00:01:15,920 --> 00:01:17,584
Stage two: requirements.

35
00:01:18,134 --> 00:01:20,801
Now the plan becomes a list of precise statements.

36
00:01:21,351 --> 00:01:23,932
Functional requirements say what the system does:

37
00:01:24,482 --> 00:01:25,783
show the product list,

38
00:01:25,913 --> 00:01:26,959
place an order.

39
00:01:27,509 --> 00:01:30,325
Non-functional requirements say how well it does it:

40
00:01:30,875 --> 00:01:32,304
answer within one second,

41
00:01:32,434 --> 00:01:33,757
keep passwords safe.

42
00:01:34,307 --> 00:01:36,781
Vague requirements are why projects drift.

43
00:01:38,831 --> 00:01:40,367
Stage three: design.

44
00:01:40,917 --> 00:01:43,349
Here the team decides the parts of the system,

45
00:01:43,479 --> 00:01:45,100
and how they talk to each other.

46
00:01:45,650 --> 00:01:47,208
CloudCart has a browser,

47
00:01:47,338 --> 00:01:48,234
an API,

48
00:01:48,364 --> 00:01:49,494
and a data store.

49
00:01:50,044 --> 00:01:51,239
The browser asks,

50
00:01:51,369 --> 00:01:52,628
the API answers,

51
00:01:52,758 --> 00:01:54,379
and the data store remembers.

52
00:01:54,929 --> 00:02:00,604
In our first version, the API is Java, running on Tomcat, and the data lives in memory.

53
00:02:01,154 --> 00:02:04,695
A real database, Postgres, comes in a later lesson.

54
00:02:05,245 --> 00:02:09,704
Drawing this first lets people spot problems while they are still cheap to fix.

55
00:02:11,754 --> 00:02:13,226
Stage four: build.

56
00:02:13,776 --> 00:02:15,845
Developers turn the design into code.

57
00:02:16,395 --> 00:02:18,230
This is real CloudCart source,

58
00:02:18,360 --> 00:02:20,707
the function that calculates an order total.

59
00:02:21,257 --> 00:02:22,537
It checks the quantity,

60
00:02:22,667 --> 00:02:23,861
finds the product,

61
00:02:23,991 --> 00:02:25,314
and adds up the price.

62
00:02:25,864 --> 00:02:28,275
Code lives in version control, called Git,

63
00:02:28,405 --> 00:02:31,029
so every change is tracked, and can be undone.

64
00:02:31,979 --> 00:02:34,261
Then a build tool, Maven, packages it.

65
00:02:35,811 --> 00:02:37,326
Stage five: test.

66
00:02:37,876 --> 00:02:41,140
Automated tests check the code every time it changes.

67
00:02:41,690 --> 00:02:43,396
Ours are written with J Unit,

68
00:02:43,526 --> 00:02:45,169
and run on GitHub Actions.

69
00:02:45,719 --> 00:02:47,810
This is a real run from our pipeline.

70
00:02:48,360 --> 00:02:50,002
Seven API tests pass,

71
00:02:50,132 --> 00:02:51,562
four order tests pass,

72
00:02:51,692 --> 00:02:53,953
eleven in total, zero failures,

73
00:02:54,083 --> 00:02:55,363
and the build succeeds.

74
00:02:55,913 --> 00:02:58,900
If even one test failed, the change would stop here,

75
00:02:59,030 --> 00:03:00,608
before any customer sees it.

76
00:03:02,158 --> 00:03:04,355
Stage six: release and deploy.

77
00:03:04,905 --> 00:03:07,679
The tested package goes onto a server, and starts.

78
00:03:08,229 --> 00:03:10,106
Our pipeline starts it on Tomcat,

79
00:03:10,236 --> 00:03:12,284
then checks it again while it is running.

80
00:03:12,834 --> 00:03:14,733
The health check answers: up.

81
00:03:15,283 --> 00:03:18,205
An order for two items totals seven thousand cents.

82
00:03:18,755 --> 00:03:20,206
Runtime checks passed.

83
00:03:20,756 --> 00:03:21,887
Passing tests,

84
00:03:22,017 --> 00:03:23,425
and a working deployment,

85
00:03:23,555 --> 00:03:24,856
are two different checks.

86
00:03:25,406 --> 00:03:26,366
You need both.

87
00:03:27,416 --> 00:03:30,808
Later, Docker and Kubernetes will do this job at scale.

88
00:03:32,358 --> 00:03:34,449
Stage seven: operate and learn.

89
00:03:34,999 --> 00:03:36,279
We watch the system,

90
00:03:36,409 --> 00:03:37,710
listen to customers,

91
00:03:37,840 --> 00:03:39,440
and plan the next improvement.

92
00:03:39,990 --> 00:03:41,611
Then the cycle starts again.

93
00:03:43,161 --> 00:03:47,065
Tools like Prometheus and Grafana help us watch a running system.

94
00:03:47,615 --> 00:03:50,474
DevOps is about making this loop fast, and safe.

95
00:03:51,024 --> 00:03:52,624
Along the way, you will learn

96
00:03:52,754 --> 00:03:53,437
Git,

97
00:03:53,567 --> 00:03:54,313
Maven,

98
00:03:54,443 --> 00:03:55,318
Jenkins,

99
00:03:55,448 --> 00:03:56,216
Docker,

100
00:03:56,346 --> 00:03:57,327
Kubernetes,

101
00:03:57,457 --> 00:03:58,396
Terraform,

102
00:03:58,526 --> 00:03:59,486
and much more.

103
00:04:01,036 --> 00:04:01,911
Quick check.

104
00:04:02,461 --> 00:04:04,893
Which stage decides what the system must do?

105
00:04:05,443 --> 00:04:08,600
Which stage catches a bad change before customers do?

106
00:04:09,150 --> 00:04:11,219
Which two checks prove an app really works?

107
00:04:11,769 --> 00:04:13,839
Pause the video, and answer out loud.

108
00:04:20,389 --> 00:04:22,735
Next lesson: requirements, in depth.

109
00:04:23,285 --> 00:04:25,163
Subscribe, so you do not miss it.
